Package: @alchemy.run/domain
Applies-to: vendor/alchemy/alchemy.run-domain-e1c3e6d.tgz
Upstream: not yet reported
Why: A lock keeps each rule as the text written at its place in the aggregate, the decision or the model. For a
  rule listed by name (`transitions: { ARule }`, `ARule: Rules.ARule`) that text is the name. A transition moved to
  a file of its own is then refused as changed, though it is the rule that was locked; and once a lock is written
  again it holds the name, so a later change to the rule where it is declared is never seen. With this the text of a
  rule written as a name is the text of what the name is declared as, through the imports that give it.
Remove-when: The library's lock follows a rule's name to its declaration. `bun run beep patches prove` then says
  this patch's test passes without it.
Test: test/alchemy-domain/04-a-rule-by-its-name/RuleByItsName.test.ts

